    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d223">

                <pb facs="0323" n="307" sameAs="frus:frus1947v04/pg307"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">864.00/6–347: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            Legation in Hungary
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential   urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-06-03T19:00:00-04:00">June 3, 1947—7 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>594. Brit propose instruct Brit Rep ACC<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d223-f1"/> address communication Soviet Acting Chairman ACC referring to para 6 <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >c</hi> revised ACC
                    <pb facs="0324" n="308" xml:id="pg308"/> statutes<anchor n="2" xml:id="d223-f2"
                    /> and, in stating that all reports agree resignation Hung PriMin resulted from transmission info to Hung
                    Govt by ACC Chairman, requesting copies all info
                    handed to Hung Govt by Soviet Chairman on behalf of Soviet authorities connected
                    Kovács case. Brit considered advisable let such action be known publicly and
                    contemplate announcing dispatch instructions to Brit Rep in reply on June 4 to
                    question tabled House of Commons.</p>
                <p>Please ask Gen Weems transmit parallel communication Acting Chairman ACC in concert his Brit colleague.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d223-f3"/> Press here will also be informed June 4.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d223-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent Budapest, rptd London and Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d223-f1" n="1" xml:id="d223-f1-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. Oliver Pearce Edgecumbe.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d223-f2" n="2" xml:id="d223-f2-content">
                <p>For the text of the Revised Statute of the Allied Control Commission for Hungary,
                    see the enclosure to the note from Marshal Voroshilov to General Key, August 14,
                    1945, <ref target="frus1945v04#pg_845">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1945, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 845</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d223-f3" n="3" xml:id="d223-f3-content">
                <p>General Weems’ communication, dated June 4, is not printed. In a reply dated June
                    9, the text of which was transmitted to the Department in telegram 1005, June
                    12, from Budapest, neither printed, General Sviridov rejected the contention
                    that the Kovács case was a matter of concern to the Allied Control Commission
                    and stated that the request for information on the matter had been turned over
                    to the appropriate Soviet authorities (864.00/6–1247).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d223-f4" n="4" xml:id="d223-f4-content">
                <p>For the statement under reference here, which was read to news correspondents on
                    June 4, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, June 15,
                    1947, p. 1161.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
